  "title": "This Aint Maradona Central Banking",
  "type": "macro",
  "priority": "medium",
  "published": "2026-08-03",
  "insights": [
   "Fed Chair Kevin Warsh's refusal to provide forward guidance is based on a flawed belief that financial markets provide 'world-class' economic advice that the Fed should follow.",
   "Warsh's strategy differs from 'Maradona central banking' (Mervyn King's concept), where the central bank manipulates market expectations to achieve objectives without actual policy moves; Warsh instead treats the market as an omniscient advisor.",
   "The market does not provide 'advice' on what the Fed should do; it merely summarizes bets on what the Fed will do based on the central bank's reaction function.",
   "Reducing forward guidance does not improve policy outcomes; it only increases market uncertainty and volatility.",
   "The bond market's historical inability to forecast interest rates or the amplitude of the business cycle suggests it cannot replace the judgment of policymakers."
  ],
  "macro": [
   {
